MID_SE_Led_Dan_C: <EDH>
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_001: Leda! Just the woman I hoped to see. Are you finally\ngoing to let me interview you?
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_002: Only because you're so, SO persistent. I mean, really,\nthere's asking and there's begging...
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_003: A good writer is like a snake. She finds a juicy\nstory—she must champ down and never let go!
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_004: Now, I'd like to hear all about you—the alluring\nassassin targeting the White-Maned Demon!
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_005: Alright, alright, calm down. *sigh* Um... I suppose I'll\nstart from my early days in Cydonia...
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_006: So, that's my story. Was that helpful?
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_007: Yes, quite! Your account is truly inspiring. It screams\nto be written down!
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_008: Who better than a master auteur to answer the\nshriek of duty?
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_009: Um... Don't you have some kind of relationship\nwith Bertrand?
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_010: You must not have many boundaries if you're using\nsomeone who tried to kill him as your subject.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_011: But that's what makes it such a good story!
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_012: The flames of hatred that seared your body and soul,\nthe unshakable drive to avenge your father.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_013: Your choice to trade a simple life for blood and sweat\nand strife... It will be catnip to my audience!
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_014: Won't your readers want something...lighter?\nChivalric stories? Fairy tales?
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_015: You don't understand. When you break a story down\nto its smallest constituent parts, what is it made of?
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_016: Letters?
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_017: Pah, no! It is made of emotions!
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_018: What the characters feel, the readers feel. Their\nhearts ache and sink and tremble!
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_019: Sorry, but...my heart didn't do any of that when I\nread The Rise of the White-Maned Demon.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_020: I found it hard to connect with the protagonist,\nobviously. But also—it was clear he'd keep winning.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_021: Whoever challenged him was sure to meet defeat\nand failure, so there was no suspense.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_022: Oh. Hm... Why, that's quite astute, Leda! I'll bear\nthat in mind.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_023: Had you said something negative without reading it,\nI'd tie you to a chair and hand you a copy.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_024: But I welcome feedback from those who honestly\nengage with my works.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_025: Hmm. That's very good-natured of you. I thought\nyou'd be furious.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_026: As a playwright, I find criticism helpful as an\nexpression of the audience's expectations.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_027: I see it won't be easy meeting yours—but meet them\nI shall. Just you wait!
MID_SE_Led_Dan_C_028: And she's gone. Unpredictable, that one.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B: <EDH>
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_001: Ehehe... It's done. I've done it.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_002: Wh-whoa, what happened?!
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_003: Oh, there you are. Immaculate timing.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_004: It's done... I have finished it. The Rose's Revenge.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_005: Dante! You don't look well... I'm gonna go get\nsomeone, OK?
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_006: *snore*
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_007: Oh... You had me worried there, but you were\njust exhausted.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_008: You worked so hard writing this thing that you\ndidn't sleep?
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_009: Well, I'd feel bad leaving her here. I guess I'll carry\nher to bed.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_010: Mm num num... Huh?
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_011: You're awake. It wasn't the easiest thing hauling\nyou back to bed, you know.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_012: Have you read it yet—my latest masterpiece?!
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_013: That's your first question? Yes, I read it.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_014: Little risky. If someone I know read it, they'd realize\ninstantly that it's about me.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_015: And? You have led a beautiful life! I myself was\nmoved to tears many times as I wrote.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_016: If you made it to the end, I can only assume you are\nstill feeling affected yourself.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_017: You'd think so. But after everything that I told you\nabout my life, I actually found it lacking.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_018: Ah, such a demanding reader! Fine, then—be my\neditor. Tell me what adjustments to make.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_019: No need—I've made them for you. Tell me what\nyou think.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_020: What do you mean?
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_021: Just listen. I'll play something for you.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_022: What did you think? I had to make a few tweaks to\nthe words to match my melody.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_023: Wonderful, Leda. Absolutely wonderful!
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_024: It was as if my words were buoyed by your\npassionate melodies.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_025: My revenge was hardly a work of art. I'm sure it was\nunbearable to see.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_026: By contrast, the protagonist of The Rose's Revenge\nhas a beautiful determination.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_027: Which...made me feel a little better. Thanks, Dante.
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_028: *gasp*
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_029: Dante?
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_030: Silence! I can hear it in my mind—the soft hum of\nan idea. A sequel is in order!
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_031: I'll have to get back to writing at once. Rejoice, Leda,\nfor this play shall very soon have a younger sibling!
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_032: I tell her my story, she writes it down, I respond\nto it, she gets excited and runs off to write another...
MID_SE_Led_Dan_B_033: The cycle may never stop. Good thing it's fun.